@database "agent_eval"
@$VER: agent_eval.guide 0.1 (26.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Evals"
@{h1}Evals

Did the new model make the agent better or worse? Did the prompt edit help,
or did one lucky run only look like it? @{b}agent_eval@{ub} answers that
with numbers: a @{b}suite@{ub} names an agent, a few variants of it
(@{b}arms@{ub}) and a set of tasks with a check each. Every arm runs every
task several times, each run in a process and a workspace of its own, and
the report compares the arms task by task -- with a verdict that says when
the difference is too small to tell from noise.

It changes no agent. It is the measuring instrument for everything that
does: a prompt edit, a model switch, a new tool.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Evals panel: three runs, one compared arm against arm"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Evals panel  " link panel}
  @{"  Reading a report  " link report}

For suite authors
  @{"  Writing a suite  " link suite}
  @{"  Checks  " link checks}
  @{"  Judging by a model  " link judge}
  @{"  Arms and overlays  " link arms}

For developers and administrators
  @{"  On the command line  " link cli}
  @{"  How a run is executed  " link execution}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Evals panel"
@{h1}The Evals panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Agents@{ub}, "Evals"). It is for
administrators only: starting a run executes the suite's checks, and the
records hold the agents' answers.

@{h2}Starting a run

The list at the top offers every suite found below an @{tt}evals/@{utt}
directory -- the project's own and every plugin's (see
@{" Writing a suite " link suite}) -- with its agent and how many agent
runs it makes. Pick one and press @{b}Run@{ub}; the
panel asks first and names how many agent runs that is, because each costs
what the agent costs. The run starts in a process of its own: it goes on
when the panel is closed or the API restarts.

@{h2}The runs

The left column lists every run, newest first: the suite, when it started,
the agent and arms, its status, how many runs passed and what they cost. A
run is @{tt}running@{utt} while its process lives, with how many of its
agent runs counted so far and a bar for it; one whose process is gone
without finishing shows @{tt}died@{utt}. @{tt}stopped@{utt} means
somebody pressed Stop before every agent run had started;
@{tt}incomplete@{utt}, that some agent runs did not count (see "Runs that
did not count"). Only a @{tt}done@{utt} run can become the baseline. The
suite's baseline carries a badge.

@{h2}One run

Click a run to read it on the right. Its head names the suite, the run id,
the agent and when it started; below it three figures: agent runs that
counted of planned (with how many did not count), passed of those, and the
cost in all and per run (with how many runs had no known cost).

@{bullet}@{b}Make baseline@{ub} (a finished run only) makes it the run
later runs of the same suite are compared against. @{b}Stop@{ub} (while
running) starts no further agent runs; those already running finish.
@{bullet}@{b}Compare against@{ub}: its own reference arm (the first arm
of the suite, run beside the others) or the suite's baseline; offered only
while the suite has a baseline other than this run. A baseline
from an older version of the suite file is compared all the same, with a
warning: a task of the same name may ask or check something else now.
Picking another run goes back to its own reference arm.
Three tabs below, the chosen one kept as the run reloads and from run to
run:

@{bullet}@{b}Arms@{ub}: runs, share passed, mean score, cost, and the cost of
a run at the 90th percentile. The mean score is the mean over tasks, each
task weighing the same, as in the verdict; below it, where the checks count
what they score (tests, expectations; a script or python check returns
@{i}of@{ui}), the pooled score: every test or expectation weighing the same
-- each run weighs the most any run of its task counted, so one whose
import broke (one error instead of the task's tests) or that timed out
met none of them; then the verdict for each arm against the
reference -- see @{" Reading a report " link report}.
@{bullet}@{b}Tasks@{ub}: a table task by task, passed of runs and the mean
score per arm.
@{bullet}@{b}Runs@{ub}: every single agent run. A click opens one above the
table with the
check's output, the end of the agent's answer, its request id (to find
its LLM calls) and its directory.

A run started from the panel whose runner ended before it set the run up
-- an arm that cannot run, a suite broken since it was offered -- shows the
runner's reason instead of "starting".

The panel reloads every 10 seconds by itself while it is visible.
@endnode

@node report "Reading a report"
@{h1}Reading a report

@{h2}Why per task

A task decides most of a run's outcome: an easy one passes for every arm, a
hard one fails for every arm. So the unit of comparison is the task, not
the run. For each task the report takes the mean score of the arm minus the
mean score of the reference, and asks: on how many tasks is the arm better,
on how many worse?

@{h2}The verdict

@{table}
Verdict | Means
better / worse | the arm is better (worse) on so many more tasks than the other way round that chance alone would do that less than one time in twenty (exact sign test, p < 0.05). The direction is the count's, never the mean's
no detectable difference | the tasks do not decide it. Not the same as "equal"
not decidable with so few tasks | with five tasks or fewer no outcome can reach p < 0.05 -- not even all of them better
no common tasks | the reference has tasks of this kind, the arm ran none of them
@{body}

Next to it:

@{bullet}How many tasks it takes: the sign test counts only the tasks
that @{b}moved@{ub} -- a task both arms score alike is a tie and does not
count either way. Of those, enough must move the same way: 6 of 6, 8 of 8,
9 of 10, 12 of 15, 15 of 20. The report names the number for the tasks that
moved. Six better and four tied decides; six better and four worse of ten
does not, however large the gain on those six. With five or fewer tasks
that moved, nothing can decide.
@{bullet}@{b}mean difference@{ub} with a 95% interval (Student's t over the
tasks): how large the effect is, and how sure. It assumes the differences
scatter roughly evenly; when every task moved by the same amount there is
no interval, and the report says "every task alike".

More tasks is the lever, not more repeats: repeats make each task's mean
steadier, but only the number of tasks decides how small an effect the
test can see. A check with a @{b}score@{ub} (the share of green tests)
rather than pass/fail helps each task's difference show at all.

@{h2}Learn, regression, holdout

Every task has a kind. @{b}learn@{ub} and @{b}holdout@{ub} tasks are
judged apart: once anyone has tuned an agent on the learn tasks, only the
holdout tasks are evidence. A @{b}regression@{ub} task is checked on its
own: one the reference passed every time and the arm passes at most a third
of the time is listed under @{b}Regressions@{ub}.

@{h2}Runs that did not count

A run that timed out or crashed scores 0 and counts -- that is what the arm
did. A run refused before it started (the agent's role gate), one never
started because the budget was spent or the run was stopped, the runs of a
task whose setup failed or timed out, and a run the runner itself failed
(a locked file; status @{tt}error@{utt}, the suite goes on) do not count;
the report lists them under "Not counted". An arm that cannot run at all (an unknown agent or profile,
an overlay that does not validate) stops the whole run before its first
agent run, instead of scoring zeros.

@{h2}Cost

Cost, calls and tokens are read from the usage tracker's records by the
run's request id -- sub-agents included, and also for a run that timed out
or crashed. Without the @{tt}context_usage_tracker@{utt} plugin, or with
its store moved elsewhere, a run's cost is unknown: the report counts such
runs apart ("+ n run(s) unknown") instead of as free, and a
@{tt}budget_usd@{utt} cannot be kept -- the runner says so once.

@{h2}Fallbacks

An agent runs on the first profile of its chain, and on the next when that
one fails -- an arm that sets a model may so have run partly on another.
The same records tell: every run lists, per agent, the models it really
called (@{tt}models@{utt}), and the run keeps, per arm, the model each agent
it names runs on first -- the suite's agent with the arm's overlay (a
task's overlay included), every agent of its @{tt}agents@{utt}. An agent
that also ran on another model is named in the report
(@{tt}fallbacks@{utt}; a warning under the arm, on the command line a
WARNING line): those runs did not measure the arm's choice alone. An agent
with @{tt}auto_escalate_on_stuck@{utt} may run on its advanced model by
design: that one is a note (@{tt}escalated@{utt}) -- with a chain of one
profile it is also where a fallback goes first, the usage cannot tell the
two apart. Without escalation, the advanced chain is one more fallback.
Compared against a baseline, a fallback in the baseline's reference is a
warning of the report too.

@{h2}A baseline from another day

Comparing against a baseline measures more than the change: providers route
differently over the day, models behind a "latest" name move. Arms of one
run started side by side, task by task, in a shuffled order -- prefer them
for an A/B, and the baseline for "is it still as good as last week".
@endnode

@node suite "Writing a suite"
@{h1}Writing a suite

A suite is a file @{tt}suite.yaml@{utt} in a directory below
@{tt}evals/@{utt}: the project's @{tt}evals/<name>/suite.yaml@{utt}, or a
plugin's @{tt}<plugin>/evals/<name>/suite.yaml@{utt} -- a plugin brings its
suites the way it brings its agents. Several suites may share a directory:
any other YAML file there that names an @{tt}agent@{utt} and
@{tt}tasks@{utt} is one too. Every relative path in it means the suite's own
directory.

@{code}
name: coder-basics              # letters, digits, '-'
agent: coder                    # the agent every arm starts from
description: "Small fixes in a toy repository"
repeats: 3                      # runs per arm and task
timeout_s: 1800                 # one agent run, then it is killed
parallel: 2                     # agent runs at the same time
budget_usd: 5                   # no new runs once this is spent
data: fresh                     # fresh (default) | shared
chain: false
arms:
  base: {}                      # the first arm is the reference
  think:
    agent_config: {llm_profile: [think]}
tasks:
  - id: fix-pager
    kind: learn                 # learn | regression | holdout
    input: "tests/test_pager.py fails. Find and fix the bug."
    workspace: pager_repo       # copied fresh into every run
    check: {pytest: tests}
  - id: capital
    kind: regression
    input: "What is the capital of France? Answer with the city only."
    check: {expect: {contains: [Paris]}}
@{body}

@{h2}The workspace

Every run gets a fresh copy of the task's @{tt}workspace@{utt} directory.
The agent is started in it, so its tools take it for "."; with
@{tt}data: fresh@{utt} it also is the data directory's
@{tt}workspace/@{utt}, where file tools point as shipped
(@{tt}data/workspace@{utt}). Tell the agent in the task where to work.

@{h2}data: fresh or shared

@{bullet}@{b}fresh@{ub}: every run gets an empty data directory of its own.
Its sessions, caches and databases stay in the run's directory -- nothing
it does touches the installation's data, and you can read its session
afterwards. The panels that read the installation's data (context and
usage, the message debugger) therefore do not show such a run; its usage
and models are in the suite's report.
@{bullet}@{b}shared@{ub}: the run uses the installation's data directory,
for an agent that has to read what is there (a database it works on).
Runs then see each other's leftovers.

@{h2}Setup: the same start for every arm

@{code}
  - id: sales-report
    input: "Write the monthly report from sales.csv in the workspace."
    setup: "agent-run --agent data_builder \"Write sample data to data/workspace/sales.csv\""
    check: {python: "my_plugin.evals.checks:sales_report"}
@{body}

A task's @{tt}setup@{utt} command runs @{b}once@{ub} (in a copy of the
workspace and, with @{tt}data: fresh@{utt}, a data directory of its own), and
its result is frozen:
every run of every arm starts from a copy of it. Generating the material
inside each run instead would give every arm different input -- and the
comparison would measure that. When the setup fails or runs past
@{tt}timeout_s@{utt}, none of the task's runs start. A plain
@{tt}python@{utt} in the command is the interpreter the runner runs on,
whether or not its environment was activated.

@{h2}A task's own agent_config

@{code}
  - id: beginner-intro
    input: "Explain recursion."
    agent_config:
      template_vars: {audience: beginners, with_examples: true}
@{body}

What a task's prompt needs and the next task's does not -- the template
variables of a templated prompt, say -- goes in the task's
@{tt}agent_config@{utt}. It is task material: every arm gets it, merged
over the agent's own @{b}before@{ub} the arm's overlay, so the arm still
decides what it changes.

@{h2}Chains

With @{tt}chain: true@{utt} each arm runs the tasks @{b}in order@{ub}, in
one workspace and data directory per repeat: every task sees what the one
before left behind. For agents whose work builds on earlier decisions. Each
repeat starts from scratch. The arms still go side by side: the next task
starts once every arm has finished the one before.

@{h2}Starting in the middle of a machine run

A stage deep in a long machine run is costly to reach: every arm would run
all the stages before it. A @{b}fork task@{ub} starts there instead -- from a
run of the machine that has already been, frozen once:

@{code}
agent-eval export-run <run_id> evals/review-stage/source --include json_store/<namespace>
@{body}

@{code}
  - id: review
    fork:
      source: source            # the export directory, next to the suite
      at: review                # the top-level state the fork starts at
      visit: 1                  # its first entry (default); 2 the second
      until: polish             # stop when the fork enters it (default: run to the end)
      definition: current       # current (default): the machine as it is now; snapshot: as it ran
      mocks: {notify: "skip"}   # over the source's: stages the task does not measure
    check: {script: check_review.py}
@{body}

Every run of every arm forks the source at the step where it entered
@{tt}at@{utt}: the steps before are replayed from its journal, at no cost,
and the machine runs on by itself from there -- a fork task has no
@{tt}input@{utt}. What an arm changes is what the machine's agents do, so
its overlays go in @{tt}agents@{utt}. An @{tt}until@{utt} that is a
composite (it has no enter of its own) stops the fork right after the step
that entered it, before the work of the state inside it.
@{tt}until@{utt} is the next entry after the fork point: forked at a state
until the same one -- or one the fork point's own step enters --, the fork
runs to where it comes again. A fork task's own
@{tt}agent_config@{utt} is refused, and so are an arm's @{tt}agent@{utt} and
@{tt}agent_config@{utt} when every task is a fork task (they change the
suite's agent, which a fork never runs; its record names no model). Once the fork enters @{tt}until@{utt}
(or waits for an event -- nobody sends one here --, also one with a
timeout; a timer state and a semaphore slot are waited out) it is
terminated and the check reads what it made up to there. The source's own breakpoints and watchpoints do
not come along.

The export is the run's row and journal (@{tt}source.json@{utt}) and, in
@{tt}data/@{utt}, every path of the data directory @{tt}--include@{utt}
names -- what the machine's fork hooks read, a store's documents. It leaves
out who ran it (user, session, run key, caller) but keeps its params, the
outputs of its steps and its definition: what a run worked on goes with it,
so keep an export of private work out of a directory that is published. Each run
gets a copy of those in its data directory, as new files. A fork task needs
@{tt}data: fresh@{utt} and no chain: the source goes into the run's own
@{tt}runs.db@{utt}. A state the source never entered there (as often as
@{tt}visit@{utt} says), or an export missing, refuses the suite, and so
does an @{tt}until@{utt} the machine lacks or never stops at (a top-level
final) with @{tt}definition: snapshot@{utt}. With
@{tt}current@{utt} the run finds out: a fork that does not start is
@{tt}refused@{utt}, not scored. Only a run that ended exports, and not one
retention compacted.
@endnode

@node checks "Checks"
@{h1}Checks

A check scores a finished run: a score between 0 and 1 and passed or not.
Exactly one kind per task.

@{table}
Check | Scores
command: "make test" | runs the command in the workspace; exit 0 passes (score 1 or 0)
pytest: "tests" | runs pytest on the given paths in the workspace; the score is the share of passed tests, passed means all
expect: {contains: [...], regex: [...]} | the agent's final answer: the share of strings found and patterns matched
script: "check.py" | runs the script (next to the suite) as @{tt}script <workspace> <run.json>@{utt}; its last line of output is @{tt}{"score": ..., "passed": ..., "detail": "..."}@{utt}
python: "module:function" | calls @{tt}function(workspace, run, task)@{utt} in the runner; returns the same dict -- the way a plugin brings checks of its own
judge: true, or judge: [ids] | the suite's judge, after all runs: yes or no on each of its criteria (or the ones named), the score the share of yes -- see @{" Judging by a model " link judge}
@{body}

Every check takes @{tt}timeout_s@{utt} (600 by default); past it the
check is ended together with everything it started, and scores 0. A check
that fails to run scores 0 and says why.

@{h2}What a check reads

The run a @{tt}script@{utt} gets as @{tt}run.json@{utt} and a
@{tt}python@{utt} check as @{tt}run@{utt} is the agent run's result: the
@{tt}answer@{utt} (all of it), @{tt}errors@{utt}, @{tt}model@{utt} (what
the suite's agent was built with) and @{tt}models@{utt} (per agent, the
models it really called and how often),
@{tt}session_id@{utt} and @{tt}request_id@{utt} (its LLM calls, sub-agents'
under @{tt}<request_id>_@{utt}), @{tt}calls@{utt}, @{tt}cost@{utt} and
@{tt}duration_s@{utt} among others, and what the agent left behind:

@{bullet}@{tt}data_dir@{utt}: the run's data directory -- the stores its
agents wrote (a JSON store, a database) are below it, as in the
installation's @{tt}data/@{utt}. With @{tt}data: shared@{utt}, the
installation's own.
@{bullet}@{tt}fork@{utt} (a fork task's): the fork's @{tt}run_id@{utt},
its @{tt}source@{utt} and @{tt}at_step@{utt}, its @{tt}status@{utt} when it
stopped, and @{tt}until_reached@{utt}.
@{bullet}@{tt}stategraph_runs@{utt}: the machine runs in that data
directory that began or moved on during this agent run -- in a chain also
one an earlier task started and this one drove on --, oldest first, each
with @{tt}id@{utt},
@{tt}machine@{utt}, @{tt}status@{utt}, @{tt}final_state@{utt},
@{tt}caller_run@{utt} (the run whose call activity started it; none for
one an agent started) and @{tt}parent_run@{utt} (the run it was forked
from); their journal is
@{tt}<data_dir>/stategraph/runs.db@{utt}. Only with @{tt}data: fresh@{utt}
(otherwise @{tt}null@{utt}): in the installation's every run since would
count.

@{h2}Reading the products

@{tt}plugins.agent_eval.evidence@{utt} reads them for a check, read-only;
each function takes the run (the parsed @{tt}run.json@{utt}):

@{table}
Function | Gives
store_doc(run, doc, namespace=None, server="json_store") | the value of a JSON store document as the run left it on disk, None when there is none; without a namespace any, refused when several have it
entered(run, run_id=None) | the states the machine run entered, in order (a composite, then the state inside it), each with @{tt}state@{utt}, its journal @{tt}key@{utt} and @{tt}ts@{utt} (UTC) -- how long a stage took is the gap
spend(run, run_id=None) | what the machine run spent, the runs it called included: @{tt}cost@{utt}, @{tt}tokens@{utt} and, once more than one state spent, @{tt}by_state@{utt} (the cost per state path, a state above its children)
machine_run(run) | the run the two above read by default: a fork task's fork, else the one machine run the agent started (refused when it started several: pass @{tt}run_id@{utt})
@{body}

A @{tt}script@{utt} or @{tt}python@{utt} check whose score is a share of
countable things -- found facts, met criteria -- returns @{tt}of@{utt}
with it, how many: the report pools by it (see
@{" The Evals panel " link panel}). pytest and expect checks do so by
themselves.

@{h2}pytest and an agent that edits its tests

An agent that rewrites the failing test passes it. So, before pytest runs:

@{bullet}every file of the workspace template under the given paths is
compared with the run's copy, and a @{tt}conftest.py@{utt} the template
does not have is refused -- either scores 0 with "test files changed by the
run";
@{bullet}only the template's test files are collected: a test file the run
adds does not count;
@{bullet}a report left from before is deleted, and the workspace is not
put first on the module path (a @{tt}pytest.py@{utt} in it cannot stand in
for pytest);
@{bullet}pytest runs confined to the workspace (its own rootdir and ini
beside it), so no conftest of the repository the runs live under takes
part.

What none of this catches: the code under test runs inside the pytest
process, and code there can do anything that process can -- end it with a
forged report, for one. For an agent you trust that is no concern. For one
you do not (a variant a model wrote), score the result in a process the
agent's code never runs in -- a @{tt}script@{utt} or @{tt}python@{utt} check
that reads files instead of importing them. The checks also run with the
runner's rights and environment, not in the agent's sandbox.
@endnode

@node judge "Judging by a model"
@{h1}Judging by a model

Some products have no check a script could run: a chapter, a synopsis, a
review. A task with @{tt}check: {judge: true}@{utt} is scored by a model
instead, the suite's @{b}judge@{ub}:

@{code}
judge:
  agent: plain_judge            # an agent without tools; its llm_profile is the judge's model
  agent_config: {llm_profile: [or-gemini-pro]}   # over it, as an arm's
  instructions: "The products are the first scene of a children's book."
  criteria:                     # ask so that yes is good
    concrete: "Does the scene show what happens through actions, not summaries?"
    voice: "Can a child of six follow every sentence?"
  product: {script: product.py} # what is judged; without it, the final answer
  timeout_s: 900
tasks:
  - id: scene-1
    input: "..."
    check: {judge: true}          # or [concrete]: only those
@{body}

@{h2}How it judges

Once every run has ended, the judge gets one call per task: the products of
every arm and every repeat of it, shuffled, labelled P1, P2, ... -- no arm
name, no order it could read --, with the task's input, the instructions
and the criteria. It answers true or false per product and criterion. A
run's score is the share of true, its @{tt}of@{utt} the number of criteria
(the pooled score counts each criterion alike); it passes with every one.
The verdicts are in the run's @{tt}verdicts@{utt}, its detail says them.

Why not a grade from 1 to 10: a grade measures how much text there is more
than what is in it. A yes or no on one feature is harder to inflate.

A product the answer leaves out, or one without a true or false for every
criterion, is not counted (@{tt}judge_failed@{utt}), and neither are the
runs of a judge call that failed; the run is then incomplete, as with a
stop, and @{tt}agent-eval judge <run_id>@{utt} judges them again. Their
cost still counts: they ran. A product that cannot be read scores 0, as a
check that fails does. A criterion may not be named like a label
(@{tt}P2@{utt}).

@{h2}The product

Without @{tt}product@{utt} the judge reads the agent's final answer. With
@{tt}script@{utt} the script is called as
@{tt}script <workspace> <run.json>@{utt} right after the run -- in a chain
before the next task changes the workspace -- and what it prints is the
product (its error output is not); with @{tt}python: "module:function"@{utt}
the function gets @{tt}(workspace, run, task)@{utt} and returns it. Either
reads what the run made with @{" the reading helpers " link checks}, a
store document, say.

@{h2}Its own family and length

A model favours text of its own family. The judge's model must therefore be
of a family none of the arms' agents runs on, nor escalates to: a run does
not start otherwise. The family is told by the model's name -- its vendor prefix and
its first word, so @{tt}~anthropic/claude-sonnet-latest@{utt} and
@{tt}claude-opus-5-5@{utt} share one; two names of one vendor without a
prefix (@{tt}gpt-6@{utt}, @{tt}o3@{utt}) are not told apart. After the
runs, the report checks again what really ran: an agent that fell back
onto the judge's family, or a judge that fell back onto an arm's, is a
warning.

Length is checked within each arm: of every two products of a task, how
often the longer one scored higher and how often lower. When the longer
wins significantly more often, the report warns that the judge may be
judging length. With @{tt}repeats: 1@{utt} there are no two, and nothing to
check.

The judge's calls cost, but not the arms: the report shows them apart.
They run after a stop and past @{tt}budget_usd@{utt} too -- the runs they
judge ran, and unjudged they would count as never run.
@endnode

@node arms "Arms and overlays"
@{h1}Arms and overlays

An arm is the suite's agent with an @{b}overlay@{ub}: changes for this run
only, the agent's YAML stays as it is.

@{code}
arms:
  base: {}
  adv:
    agent_config: {llm_profile: [code_adv]}
  terse:
    agent_config:
      system_prompt: "Answer in one sentence."
      tools: {allowed: ["!terminal/*"]}
  other-agent:
    agent: coder_v2
@{body}

@{tt}agent_config@{utt} merges over the agent's own like a config entry over the
one it inherits from: mappings merge, @{tt}+x@{utt} adds to a list,
@{tt}!x@{utt} removes from it, a plain list replaces it, and
@{tt}key: null@{utt} removes what the agent has there (the field's
default applies) -- the way to swap the model of an agent whose
@{tt}llm_params@{utt} are keyed by its profile:
@{tt}{llm_profile: [other], llm_params: {own_profile: null}}@{utt}. Below a
key the agent does not have, a null is a value, as in an agent YAML
(@{tt}max_tokens: null@{utt}: the provider's default). An inline
@{tt}system_prompt@{utt} wins over the agent's @{tt}system_template@{utt}.
A @{tt}system_template@{utt} starting with @{tt}./@{utt} or @{tt}../@{utt}
means the suite's directory, as in an agent YAML it means the YAML's: the
prompt as it was before an edit, kept next to the suite, makes the
reference arm of an A/B on the edit. A file that is not there refuses the
suite. @{tt}agent@{utt} runs another agent altogether. Each run records the model it ran
on, so you can see an arm's profile took effect.

@{h2}Overlays for the agents it starts

@{tt}agent_config@{utt} reaches the suite's agent only. An agent that hands
its work on -- a machine that runs a panel, an agent that spawns
sub-agents -- runs those with their own configuration. @{tt}agents@{utt}
names them:

@{code}
arms:
  base: {}
  strict-review:
    agents:
      reviewer: {llm_profile: [review_alt]}
      reviewer_panel: {template_vars: {strictness: high}}
@{body}

Each is laid over the named agent's configuration before anything is built
from it, so every run of that agent within the arm's runs gets it, however
deep in the chain. Merged like @{tt}agent_config@{utt}, over what the agent
resolves to. Every agent that inherits from a named one gets its overlay
too -- name a parent, and you change its heirs; a named heir gets both, in
whatever order they are listed. A name that is no agent of the
configuration, an overlay that does not validate or an unknown profile
refuses the suite.

@{h2}Admin overlays and machine overlays

A suite is written by an administrator, so its overlays may set anything
the agent's YAML may set. An overlay a @{b}model@{ub} writes -- a tool
proposing a variant of an agent -- is checked by
@{tt}machine_overlay_problems()@{utt} before it may run: it may only set
@{tt}system_prompt@{utt}, @{tt}template_vars@{utt} (strings),
@{tt}skills@{utt}, @{tt}tools.allowed@{utt} / @{tt}tools.blocked@{utt} and
@{tt}llm_params@{utt} (temperature; thinking level and max tokens at most
the agent's own), and the result may only narrow: no tool allowed that was
not, no block lifted, no skill added. Hooks, templates (a path), profiles
and every other field are refused.
@endnode

@node cli "On the command line"
@{h1}On the command line

@{code}
agent-eval run <suite.yaml> [--arm a --arm b] [--baseline]
agent-eval list
agent-eval show <run_id> [--against <run_id> | --against-baseline] [--json]
agent-eval baseline <run_id>
agent-eval stop <run_id>
agent-eval export-run <run_id> <dir> [--data <dir>] [--include <path> ...]
agent-eval judge <run_id>
@{body}

@{tt}python -m plugins.agent_eval@{utt} is the same before the command is
installed. @{tt}run@{utt} first checks that every arm can run (the agent
exists, the overlay validates, its profiles exist) and refuses the suite
otherwise; then it prints a line per agent run and the report at the end
-- for a run of one arm against the suite's baseline, if it has one, for
several arms against their own reference arm. @{tt}--arm@{utt} picks arms
and keeps the suite's order, so the first picked of the suite stays the
reference. @{tt}--baseline@{utt} makes the finished run the baseline (not a
stopped or incomplete one). @{tt}show --json@{utt} gives the report as
the panel reads it. @{tt}export-run@{utt} freezes a stategraph run for fork
tasks (see @{" Writing a suite " link suite}); @{tt}--data@{utt} reads it from another data
directory than the installation's -- a copy of another host's.
@{tt}judge@{utt} judges again what a run's judge left unjudged -- its runner
ended while judging, a judge call failed -- with the suite file as it ran
(refused once the file changed), and prints the report.
@endnode

@node execution "How a run is executed"
@{h1}How a run is executed

@{tt}run_suite@{utt} (@{tt}runner.py@{utt}) is the one place; the panel
starts the command line, as a process of its own.

@{bullet}Order: for every repeat and task the arms start next to each
other in a shuffled order, so they meet the same provider load. With
@{tt}parallel@{utt} several run at once; with @{tt}chain@{utt} a task starts
once every arm finished the one before.
@{bullet}Each agent run is a child process
(@{tt}python -m plugins.agent_eval.child@{utt}) started in its workspace,
with the run's data directory in @{tt}AGENT_DATA_DIR@{utt}. It drives
@{tt}agent-run@{utt}'s own code, so a session is opened, run and saved as
@{tt}agent-run@{utt} does. The arm's @{tt}agents@{utt} are laid over the
configuration as it is loaded, before the registry is built from it; the
overlay of the suite's agent reaches it through the reload path
@{tt}agent-cli reload@{utt} takes. Runs are stored under the local
operator's user (@{tt}cli_user@{utt}), whom the agent role gate trusts for
a local process.
@{bullet}Cost, calls and tokens are read afterwards from the
@{tt}context_usage_tracker@{utt}'s store by the run's request id and the
ids of its sub-requests -- so a run killed at its timeout is charged too.
Without that plugin, or with its store moved, they are unknown.
@{bullet}@{tt}budget_usd@{utt} is checked before each agent run starts:
with @{tt}parallel@{utt} the runs already in flight may still go over it.
@{bullet}A run over @{tt}timeout_s@{utt} is killed with its process group.
A process that left the group on purpose -- a terminal command in a
session or job of its own -- can outlive it. Stop only keeps new runs from
starting.
@{bullet}A failure of the runner itself in one agent run (a locked file, a
broken result) becomes that run's @{tt}error@{utt} record; the suite goes
on.
@{bullet}Started from the panel, the runner and its agent runs have no
console window; they read the configuration the API was started with.

On disk, below the data directory:

@{code}
agent_eval/runs/<run_id>/
    meta.json        suite, agent, arms, status
    suite.yaml       the suite as it was run
    records.jsonl    one line per agent run
    setup/<task>/    a task's frozen setup
    runs/<n>-<arm>-r<rep>/   workspace, data, job, result and log of the child
    runs/chain-<arm>-r<rep>/ the same for a chain, one per arm and repeat
agent_eval/baselines/<suite>.txt the suite's baseline run id
agent_eval/logs/<run_id>.log the runner's output when the panel started it (and .pid)
@{body}

Runs are not deleted by anything; remove a run's directory when you no
longer need it.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

@{code}
plugins:
  servers:
    agent_eval:
      type: agent_eval
      enabled: true
@{body}

The panel's routes are admin-only by a rule in
@{tt}config/security.yaml@{utt} (@{tt}/plugins/agent_eval/*@{utt}). The
plugin has no settings and gives agents no tools. The example suite
@{tt}src/plugins/agent_eval/evals/hello/@{utt} runs a small file-writing
agent against two tasks:

@{code}
agent-eval run src/plugins/agent_eval/evals/hello/suite.yaml
@{body}
@endnode
